<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Khanate of Maku</h1><p class="st-meta">1747 CE – 1922 CE (approx.) · capital Maku</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The longest-lived of the Azerbaijani khanates: a mountain domain on the Ottoman and Russian borders whose khans kept local power until 1922.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maku">Maku</a>, a town built under a cliff in the far north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-ararat">Mount Ararat</a>, came under the hereditary khans of the Turkic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayat-tribe">Bayat</a> clan after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> in 1747. Its strategic position on the routes to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> gave it importance beyond its size.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Unlike most khanates of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maku">Maku</a> kept its own khans under <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran</a>, which treated them as hereditary governors of a frontier district. They controlled tribes, customs posts and border trade. In the early 20th century Morteza Qoli Khan, known as Eqbal al-Saltaneh, was one of the richest and most powerful men in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, opposing the constitutionalists of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Khan's</a> government reasserted central control, and in 1922 Eqbal al-Saltaneh lost his position; he died in detention soon after. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maku">Maku</a> was integrated into the provincial system of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pahlavi-iran">Pahlavi Iran</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/khanate-of-maku.jpg" alt="Khanate of Maku" width="320" height="339"><figcaption>Image: Sword313 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maku_xanl%C4%B1%C4%9F%C4%B1_(aze).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1747 CE – 1922 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Maku</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Eqbal al-Saltaneh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Azerbaijani Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Iran</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Maku Khanate</dd></dl></aside></div>
